// @emit-error indexed-assignment target is not an array
// THE COMPOSITE-ANNOTATION HOLE IN THE CAPTURE-BOX REWRITE.
// A local mutated by a closure is boxed into a length-1 list, and `emit_rewrite` converts
// its annotation to the array form by appending `[]` to the SPELLING. That concat is an
// array spelling only for an ATOM: `"i32 | string" + "[]"` is `"i32 | string[]"`, which
// denotes `i32 | (string[])` — a union whose second arm is a list, not a list of the union.
//
// The concat has always been malformed here, and it was HARMLESS because both halves were
// wrong together: the name parsed back to the union, the recorded type held the union, the
// indexed-assignment lowering saw a non-array and rejected loudly. This file pins that
// reject.
//
// A destringify slice then handed the CONSTRUCTED array type (`mkArrayTy` over the
// element) to the same node while leaving the malformed name — fixing one half and not the
// other, which is worse than fixing neither. Every array-shaped check began passing while
// the name-keyed lowering still read `"i32 | string[]"`, and the disagreement moved into
// codegen: this file went from the reject below to CHECK-CLEAN INVALID WASM
// (`type mismatch: expected (ref $type), found (ref $type)`).
//
// The hand-over is now gated on the spelling being a single top-level atom. `i64` / `f32` /
// `f64` / `string` — every annotation `capture-mutation-nonI32.vl` covers — are atoms whose
// concat is well-formed, which is exactly why the corpus had nothing that caught this.
//
// Widening the reject into working code is a separate slice: a parenthesized
// `(i32 | string)[]` does compile and run, and that is a capability change needing its own
// fuzz gate, not a side effect of a destringify hand-over.
function outer(): i32 {
  let u: i32 | string = 1
  const f = () => {
    u = "z"
  }
  f()
  if u is string { 1 } else { 0 }
}
print(outer())
